"h":0.016523,"s":0.9547}]},"avg_confidence":0.9432,"error_message":null},{"document_type":"program","document_slug":"1970-08-20-milwaukee-mile-wi-program","document_title":"Milwaukee Mile Program — August 20, 1970","publication_year":1970,"page_label":"004.jpg","page_number":4,"storage_path":"programs/1970-08-20-milwaukee-mile-wi-program/004.jpg","mime_type":"image/jpeg","status":"complete","ocr_engine":"PaddleOCR","ocr_model":"PP-OCRv5_mobile_det + en_PP-OCRv5_mobile_rec","ocr_text":"=== COLUMN 1 ===\nMT MT P A ESII\nRACING MEANS ACTION\nRuss Lake Photo\nNearly a million miles of racing experience has been logged by these three veterans of the Milwaukee Mile. Also, each represents a dif-\nferent factory team. Bunched are Don White (#3-1969 Dodge Daytona), Norm Nelson (#41-1970 Plymouth Road Runner) and A. J. Foyt (#2-\n1969 Ford Torino. Between them, they have won five USAC late model championships in the past 11 years.\nPaul Gohde Photo\nWhen both Roger McCluskey (#1-\n1970 Plymouth SuperBird)and Norm\nNelson (#41-1970 Plymouth Road\nRunner) both pitted on the same lap,\nNelson's pit crew, which handles both\ncars, was anything but prepared.\nTheir plans went amiss when Nelson's\ncar ran too low on gas to make an-\nother lap in July's \"Miller 200.\"\nGeorge Kaprelian Photo\nNow a bit of rarity in late model\nracing, the full sized car has been\nreplaced by the smaller intermediate.\nA big 1968 Ford Galaxie was handled\nin last July's race by Indianapolis\ndriver Johnny Rutherford, who fin-\nished 15th, 12 laps off the pace.\nArmin Krueger Photo\nThe variety in USAC\nlate model racing is\napparent in this photo\nwith intermediates,\nfull-sized, pony and\nwinged cars all in\none race.\n2OO MILE STOCK CAR RACE\nPage Four\n\n=== COLUMN 2 ===\n1970\nWAUKESHA CHRYSLER-PLYMOUTH\n2141 E. MORELAND Blvd.\nWAUKESHA\nSALES • PARTS • SERVICE\nCHRYSLER-PLYMOUTH\n& HI-PERFORMANCE SPECIALISTS\nHeadquarters for Hi-Performance\nPhone: (414) 547-1761\nCars In Waukesha","ocr_json":{